# TypeScript libraries

Application code imports runtime classes from `@qlever-llc/trellis` and generated descriptors or participants from the project-local `.trellis/ts` tree.

```ts
import { TrellisClient } from "@qlever-llc/trellis";
import { participant } from "../../.trellis/ts/participants/orders-app/mod.ts";

const client = await TrellisClient.connect({
  participant,
  trellisUrl: "https://trellis.example.com",
  auth: "browser",
}).orThrow();
```

Do not construct API or participant metadata in TypeScript. Author it in native IDL, run `trellis generate`, and treat generated files as disposable build products.

Use `@qlever-llc/trellis/service/deno` or `/service/node` for service bootstrap.
Pass `participant` and the immutable provisioned `identity` to
`TrellisService.connect(...)`, plus a durable authorization-context store. Use
the connected service's generated handlers and `kv`, `store`, and `jobs` handles.
The browser-safe root does not expose native filesystem helpers.

Connected clients expose only the actions selected by their generated
participant. RPCs return `Result`-style values; `.orThrow()` is convenient at an
application boundary, while handlers should model expected failures explicitly.
Do not import provider source to call a service: install its canonical API,
select the required actions in native IDL, and generate your own local SDK.

Find exact signatures through [/api](/api). Generate after every IDL change
before checking extracted handler types.
